A||||9||olympic||My new wheels would like to thank DARKLORD!!||||14||
Z||000000||olympic||10-16-2000||02:33 AM||||  Thanks for the wheel polishing info on your site(I found a link in a search).  Very informative.  For anyone in Canada wanting to polish your wheels you can get the supplies from Princess Auto.  6" buffing wheels-$10 ea, 4 piece buffing compound kit-$12.<P>   Oh, and one small tip.  If your wheels have big flat spokes like mine you can use a palm sander to save alot of elbow grease.  I was concerned about wet sanding with an electric sander but it worked really well.  The sand paper doesn't last very long though.  I could only do about 3 spokes per 1/4 sheet before it was wore out.  And of course, a pic.......<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/wheel_6.jpg"> <BR>Thanks again!||||reg||14
Z||000001||Raydar||10-16-2000||09:34 AM||||Olympic.<BR>Are you serious about using an electric sander to wet sand? <BR>Please tell me it was battery-powered.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>Having been on the receiving end of a really nasty shock a few years back, this just sets my teeth on edge. <BR>Very nice job, though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||10-16-2000||10:54 AM||||Nah, I used a palm sander for that too. But I didn't exactly submerse the whole thing..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||10-16-2000||11:58 AM||||Well... More power to ya'.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>J/K!! <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fropuff||10-16-2000||04:46 PM||||Is that a T-Bird?  Those wheels look nice.  I sanded almost all my GT with a power hand sander.  Nothing happened.  As long as you don't drop in the bucket of water.  That would ruin your day.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||10-16-2000||07:21 PM||||Weelllll....<BR>Alrighty, then. My apologies to the folks who have successfully used an electric sander to wet-sand. Never occurred to me that it was possible. Never even crossed my mind. Of course, I recognize that some sanders may actually <I>be designed</I> for wet sanding. Just never thought about it. <BR>Live and learn.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||11
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||10-16-2000||07:54 PM||||Glad the Fiero Project was able to help someone out. I didn't write the article though.. I just posted it. Raydar gets the credit for sending it to me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||14
Z||000007||olympic||10-16-2000||10:26 PM|||| Yeah, the guy at the hardware store looked at me kinda funny when I told him what I was going to do with the palm sander    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>   I held the sander at a 45 degree angle and then I used a  polmolive(sp) dish soap bottle to carefully squirt water onto the sand paper to clean off the aluminum dust.  Then I had a garden hoze with a nozzle to spray off the wheel itself.  The sander exhausts its cooling air right above the sanding pad so any water that splashed up would get blown out.   I  worked on a sloped driveway so I didn't end up standing in a pool of water.<P>   The palm sander worked real well and left a perfectly sanded surface to buff.  The only trade off was I went through ALOT of sandpaper.  It took three 1/4 sheets of 220 and 600 grit per wheel.  The 600 grit had to be changed every 2-3 spokes because the grit would wear off and the "oil" that makes the paper waterproof would grind itself into the wheel leaving dark splotches.  But hey, sandpaper is cheap. <P>  I freaked when I couldn't clean the buffing compound off of the tire sidewalls with soap and water.  I thought I had wrecked a brand new set of Comp T/A's.   But then I tried some WD-40 and the compound came right off and the tires look like new. <P>Fropuff:  nope, its not a T-bird it's a 94.....<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/left16.jpg">  <P>  Those wheels are OEM 16" 1999 Cougar units and this is the start of my project code named "<B>TEMPO</B>rary Insanity"<P>Stage 1 was the 16" wheels and 215/50VR16 tires.<P>Stage 2 was the addition of a....<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/endlink.jpg">  <BR>3/4" front and rear made by addco.  They(and the wheels/tires) made an unbelievable difference in the handling and the ride is still smooth.<P>Stage 3 will be  the install of <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/brakekits_off.gif">  <BR>10" rear(already purchased) and 12" front adapted from a Taurus SHO.<P>And finally, stage 4 will be a slightly modded 250hp.....<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/shomotor.gif">  <P>  This should make for an interesting daily driver.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Feel free to poke fun, I get alot of it from my friends too...lol    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by olympic (edited 10-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||10-17-2000||11:42 PM||||Olympic, <BR>Not gonna poke fun at all. Most folks think that Fieros are waaayyyy more eccentric than a Tempo. <BR>Nice looking project. The SHO ought to make it just plain <I>scary</I> fast. <BR>Seems like I heard about someone doing this swap, before. Is it a bolt in, or do you have to do a lot of fabrication?<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-18-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000009||olympic||10-18-2000||12:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, lucidatypewriter">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Is it a bolt in, or do you have to do a lot of fabrication?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>  Well, the SHO 5 speed is just a beefed up Tempo 5 speed.  So the tranny should be a bolt-in.  Some fabrication will be needed to mount the engine.  The SHO mounts its drivetrain on a removable cradle just like the Fiero.  The Tempo has no such cradle so I may have to mount the engine with the Tempo mounts or bolt/weld in some cross members to use the SHO mounts. <P>   Space won't be a concern as the Tempo engine bay is only about 1-2" smaller in either direction than the Taurus.  I plan on purchasing a complete donor car to make the swap as painless as possible.  I considered the sweet-as-h*ll 32V V8 from the 96+ SHO but  with OBD II to deal with and automatic trans only, it just wasn't an option.||||reg||1
